"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[74345],{974345:e=>{e.exports="# Time Travel & Clones\n\nSnowflake Time Travel (`AT` / `BEFORE`), data retention, Fail-safe, and zero-copy `CLONE` → DBSQL Delta time travel and `CLONE`.\n\n> Retention tuning sits on the Delta table — `delta.logRetentionDuration` and `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` — not on a top-level Snowflake-style `DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS` knob.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Time Travel — historical queries\n\nSnowflake `AT` / `BEFORE` clauses query historical state by timestamp, statement (query id), or offset. DBSQL Delta supports `TIMESTAMP AS OF` and `VERSION AS OF`.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: timestamp\nSELECT * FROM my_table AT(TIMESTAMP => '2024-01-01 00:00:00'::TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- Snowflake: by statement (query id)\nSELECT * FROM my_table BEFORE(STATEMENT => '01234567-89ab-cdef-0123-456789abcdef');\n\n-- Snowflake: offset (5 minutes ago)\nSELECT * FROM my_table AT(OFFSET => -60 * 5);\n\n-- DBSQL: timestamp\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01 00:00:00';\n\n-- DBSQL: version (from DESCRIBE HISTORY)\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table VERSION AS OF 42;\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- Snowflake `BEFORE(STATEMENT => ...)` uses a Snowflake query id. DBSQL has no equivalent — translate to a `VERSION AS OF N` after looking up the version via `DESCRIBE HISTORY my_catalog.my_schema.my_table`.\n- Snowflake `OFFSET => -seconds` (relative time) — DBSQL has no offset form; use `TIMESTAMP AS OF current_timestamp() - INTERVAL 5 MINUTES` or compute the literal in the application layer.\n\n### Retention configuration\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: set retention\nALTER TABLE my_schema.my_table SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 30;\n\n-- DBSQL: set retention via Delta table properties\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (\n  'delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days',\n  'delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days'\n);\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- Snowflake `DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS` is a single knob (0–90 days, higher on Enterprise+). DBSQL uses two separate knobs:\n  - `delta.logRetentionDuration` — how long the transaction log entries are retained (controls `VERSION AS OF` reach).\n  - `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` — how long tombstoned data files are retained on storage (controls `TIMESTAMP AS OF` reach for restore).\n    Set both to the desired retention to mirror Snowflake's single-knob behavior.\n- DBSQL `VACUUM` is required to reclaim storage for files older than `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`. Snowflake handles this automatically.\n\n### Fail-safe\n\nSnowflake provides 7 days of `Fail-safe` recovery after Time Travel ends — Snowflake support can recover the table during this window. **DBSQL has no Fail-safe analog.** Backup-and-recover via `DEEP CLONE` to a separate catalog or external storage if disaster recovery beyond the Delta retention window is required.\n\n### Zero-copy CLONE\n\nSnowflake `CREATE TABLE x CLONE y` produces a metadata-only copy that shares underlying data files until written. DBSQL has `SHALLOW CLONE` (metadata-only) and `DEEP CLONE` (full data copy).\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: zero-copy clone (metadata-only; copy-on-write)\nCREATE TABLE my_db.my_schema.snapshot CLONE my_db.my_schema.production;\n\n-- Snowflake: clone at a historical point\nCREATE TABLE my_db.my_schema.snapshot CLONE my_db.my_schema.production\nAT(TIMESTAMP => '2024-01-01 00:00:00'::TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- DBSQL: shallow clone (metadata-only — same semantics as Snowflake CLONE)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.snapshot SHALLOW CLONE my_catalog.my_schema.production;\n\n-- DBSQL: shallow clone at a historical version (use VERSION AS OF or TIMESTAMP AS OF)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.snapshot SHALLOW CLONE my_catalog.my_schema.production\nTIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01 00:00:00';\n\n-- DBSQL: deep clone (full copy — use for cross-region/account backups)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.dr_schema.snapshot DEEP CLONE my_catalog.my_schema.production;\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- Snowflake clones extend across **databases, schemas, and entire databases** (e.g. `CREATE DATABASE dev CLONE prod`). DBSQL `CLONE` operates at the **table** level. To clone a schema or catalog, iterate over tables in the application layer (Python + Databricks SDK).\n- Snowflake clones inherit Time Travel state; DBSQL `SHALLOW CLONE` likewise references the source's underlying data files but the cloned table starts at version 0 in its own log.\n- Snowflake clones can be modified independently after creation (true copy-on-write); DBSQL `SHALLOW CLONE` writes diverge from the source after the first write to either side, but the underlying files of the **source** remain shared until the source itself is `OPTIMIZE`d or rewritten — make sure retention is wide enough on the source if the clone is read for an extended period.\n\n### Snowflake `UNDROP` → DBSQL `RESTORE`\n\nSnowflake `UNDROP TABLE` reverses an accidental drop within the retention window. DBSQL has no `UNDROP`, but a dropped Delta table's data files exist for `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` and can be restored from history.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nUNDROP TABLE my_schema.my_table;\n\n-- DBSQL: re-create the table pointing at the same path, then restore history\n-- (only works if the data files have not been VACUUMed)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table USING DELTA LOCATION '<original-path>';\nRESTORE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table TO VERSION AS OF <last-good-version>;\n```\n\nFor workflows that rely on `UNDROP` for accident recovery, set up a `DEEP CLONE` snapshot schedule instead — there is no in-place safety net comparable to Snowflake's Time Travel `UNDROP`.\n"}}]);
